plus(355,2,'One historian says an event came about through Napoleon’s power, another through Alexander’s, a third through the power of somebody else. Even when they agree on the man, they disagree about the source of his authority. Thiers, a Bonapartist, traces Napoleon’s power to his virtue and genius. Lanfrey, a Republican, traces it to the tricks with which he deceived the people. In demolishing one another’s accounts, these historians demolish their own conception of the force that brings events about. The essential question of history still has no answer.')
plus(355,4,'First the historical figure is the product of his time and his power the outcome of many forces. Next his power is itself the force that makes things happen. Gervinus, Schlosser and others at one point prove that Napoleon was a product of the Revolution, the ideas of 1789, and so on. At another they tell us outright that the campaign of 1812 and the other things they dislike were simply the product of Napoleon’s misguided will, and that a whim of his arrested the development of those very ideas of 1789. The ideas of the Revolution and the spirit of the age produced Napoleon’s power. But Napoleon’s power suppressed the ideas of the Revolution and the spirit of the age.')
plus(365,12,'The new astronomy said: “True, we cannot feel the earth move. But assume it stands still and we arrive at absurdity. Admit that it moves, though we cannot feel it, and we arrive at laws.” So the new history says: “True, we are unaware of our dependence. But assume that our will is free and we arrive at absurdity. Admit that we depend on the world outside us, on time and on cause, and we arrive at laws.”')
plus(360,14,'The proof is decisive. Give any number of commands: unless there are other causes for the event, it will never take place. But let an event occur, whatever it may be, and among the wishes people express all the time we can always find some that fit it, in what they said and when they said it, well enough to pass for commands.')
e(352,32,modern(352,32).split('Besides this feeling')[0]+'This feeling absorbed her and kept her from following the details of his plans. Other thoughts, quite unconnected with what he was saying, flitted through her mind. She thought of her nephew. Her husband’s account of how agitated the boy had been while Pierre was speaking struck her forcibly. She remembered one little trait after another of his gentle, sensitive nature, and from her nephew her thoughts went to her own children. She was not comparing them with him. She was comparing what she felt for them with what she felt for him, and was sorry to find that something was wanting in her love for young Nicholas.')
e(363,5,modern(363,5).split('But the moment I was asking')[0]+'But I am not now refraining from lifting it at the first moment, when I asked the question. Time has passed and I could not hold it still. The arm I raised then is no longer the arm I now leave at my side. Even the air I raised it in is not the air around me now. The instant of that first movement is gone for good. At that instant I could make only one movement, and whatever I did would be that one. Leaving my arm at my side a moment later does not prove I could have left it there then. And since I could make only one movement at that single instant, it could not have been another. To imagine it free I must imagine it in the present, on the boundary between past and future—in other words, outside time, which is impossible.')
e(358,19,'If God gives a command, expressing His will as the ancient histories tell us, that expression of will stands outside time and nothing causes it, for no event governs the Deity. Human beings, on the other hand, issue commands in time and in relation to one another. To explain how their commands connect with events, we must bring two things back into the account. First, the condition of everything that happens: both events and the person giving the orders move continuously through time. Second, the necessary connection between the person giving an order and those who carry it out.')
