e(362,11,modern(362,11).split('And conversely')[0]+'To someone who knows nothing about the drowning man, the hungry mother or the soldier in the ranks, each of them seems freer. A man who committed murder twenty years ago and has lived peaceably and harmlessly ever since looks less guilty, his action more subject to necessity, to someone who considers it after twenty years than to someone considering it the day after the murder. Likewise, an act committed in madness, drunkenness or violent excitement seems less free and more inevitable to someone acquainted with the person’s state of mind, freer and less inevitable to someone who knows nothing of it. In each case our idea of freedom grows or diminishes, our idea of compulsion diminishes or grows, according to the point from which we view the act. The greater the necessity we see, the less the freedom, and vice versa.')
e(355,7,modern(355,7).split('obviously does not equal')[0]+'does not add up to the result: millions of French people submitted to the Bourbons. Words exchanged by Chateaubriand, Madame de Staël and the others affected their relations with one another. They do not tell us why millions submitted. To explain how these exchanges led to mass submission—how component forces adding up to A produced a force a thousand times A—the historian falls back on the very force he denied: power. Calling it the resultant of the forces amounts to admitting an unexplained force acting on the resultant. That is exactly what the universal historians do. They contradict the specialist historians, and themselves as well.')
plus(355,10,'Nor do the savage wars and executions that followed the preaching of love.')
e(355,11,modern(355,11).split('But how this new force')[0]+'Its connection with the mass of the people needs explaining. We can understand the claim that Napoleon held power and events followed. With some effort we can even imagine that Napoleon together with other influences brought an event about. But how did a book, Le Contrat social, make Frenchmen begin drowning one another? Until someone explains the link between this new force and the event, we cannot understand it.')
